      "title": "Trump's many moods as midterm election results throw up 'blue wave'",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/trump-s-many-moods-as-midterm-election-results-throw-up-blue-wave-126110402148_1.html",
      "description": "It was a tough election night for US President Donald Trump as he lashed out at Republican candidates and even the media for the political reversals in the midterm elections.\nSome of his reactions, reflected in Truth Social posts, were balanced, while others appeared to just vent off steam as poll results trickled in.\n\"The Fox News poll, always the worst, is so insulting with the questions they ask. Every question is a hit job, and every answer is BULLSHIT! This has been going on for years,\" Trump said about his favourite news channel.\nTrump, who addressed 30 rallies urging voters to pretend that he was on the ballot, did a U-turn and blamed the electoral losses on the Republican candidates.\n\"I wasn't on the ballot, and if I was, we would have won IN A LANDSLIDE,\" Trump said as the trends indicated a wave in favour of the Democrats.\nAs the Republicans lost Texas, a strong red state, to the Democrats, Trump targeted outgoing Governor Gregg Abbott and questioned his commitment to th",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-05T02:17:27"
    },
    {
      "title": "Trump ducks blame for Republican losses in a familiar pattern for him",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/trump-ducking-blame-for-republican-losses-it-s-familiar-pattern-for-him-126110402136_1.html",
      "description": "After a flurry of rallies where he implored supporters to pretend that he was on the ballot, President Donald Trump led his Republican Party to a stunningly complete wipeout in the midterm elections.\nNow he may work just as tirelessly to insist it wasn't his fault.\nRepublican losses swept Democrats into control of Congress with a wave of Senate victories that once seemed far-fetched in GOP strongholds. There were wins in South Carolina and Kansas, which had not elected a Democratic senator since 1932. In Texas, Democrats had been shut out of every statewide race for more than three decades, until Tuesday.\nTrump was already backpedaling even before the full extent of his party's defeat was known. On election night, he sounded more humble than usual, pledging to work with Democrats and to accept the election results. But he was still seeking to deflect responsibility.\n\"I don't like to consider it a defeat for me. I wasn't on the ballot,\" he told Fox News. Voters, he said, just \"didn'",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-05T02:17:14"
    }
